    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm hitting a constant CrashLoopBackOff on a microservice in our gke cluster. i've checked the basic logs but it's not super clear why it's failing right after startup. can you give me a diagnostic checklist to help me figure out if this is a resource issue, a liveness/readiness probe failure, or something in the config? \n\nhere's a snippet of the logs:\n[stderr] error: failed to parse config: unexpected EOF\n[stderr] connection refused: listening on port 8080 failed",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"unexpected EOF\" error suggests your config file is malformed or truncated, which prevents the application from initializing even before memory issues could occur. Since the app fails to parse its configuration, any liveness or readiness probes attempting to connect to port 8080 will naturally fail, causing the `CrashLoopBackOff` loop. You should first validate your YAML configuration syntax and ensure your service image exposes the correct port matching your app's internal binding before checking resource limits or probe timeouts in Kubernetes.",
      "source": "target",
